    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nBesides polynomials, exponential and trigonometric functions are perhaps the most efficient functions in terms of calculating their definite integrals. Indeed, recall that the fundamental theorem of calculus states the following:\n\nContent:\n> If [MATH: f(x)] is a function that's continuous on an interval [MATH: [a,b]], and there exists a function [MATH: F(x)] such that [MATH: F'(x) = f(x)] on [MATH: [a,b]], then\n> [MATH: \\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = F(b) - F(a).]\n\nContent:\nTo illustrate this, let's calculate\n[MATH: \\int_1^3 2^x \\,\\textrm{d}x.]\n\nContent:\nRecall that the antiderivative of [MATH: 2^x] is [MATH: \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln{2}}.] Therefore, evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{3}2^{x}dx|= (2^{x})/(\\ln 2)|_{1}^{3}; = (1)/(\\ln 2)(2^{3} \u2212 2^{1}); = (1)/(\\ln 2)(8 \u2212 2); = (6)/(\\ln 2).]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Evaluating the Definite Integral of an Exponential Function\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^5 e^x \\, \\textrm d x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTaking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n[MATH: \u222b_{0}^{5}e^{x}dx|= e^{x}|_{0}^{5}; = e^{5} \u2212 e^{0}; = e^{5} \u2212 1.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u222b_{0}^{1}5^{x}dx.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (4)/(\\ln 5)]\nb. [MATH: 4]\nc. [MATH: 4\\ln 5]\nd. [MATH: \\ln 5 \u2212 \\ln 1]\ne. [MATH: \\ln 1 \u2212 \\ln 0]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b_{0}^{2}e^{x}dx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: e^{2} \u2212 e]\nb. [MATH: e^{2}]\nc. [MATH: e]\nd. [MATH: e^{2} \u2212 1]\ne. [MATH: 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Trigonometric Function\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\cos x \\,\\textrm{d} x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTaking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n[MATH: \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0/2}\\cos xdx|= \\sin x|_{0}^{\u03c0/2}; = \\sin ((\u03c0)/(2)) \u2212 \\sin (0); = 1 \u2212 0; = 1.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u222b_{\u2212 \u03c0/6}^{\u03c0/4}\\sec^{2} xdx.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (sqrt(3) + 1)/(sqrt(3))]\nb. [MATH: (3)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(2)]\nd. [MATH: (sqrt(2) + 1)/(sqrt(2))]\ne. [MATH: (2)/(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0/3}\\cos xdx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(2)]\nb. [MATH: (sqrt(3) \u2212 1)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: (sqrt(3))/(2)]\nd. [MATH: (1 \u2212 sqrt(3))/(2)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (sqrt(3))/(2)]"
